      ":question/worked-solution": "An equilibrium is a constant function $y(x)=c$. Set $dy/dx=0$ and test the resulting equation.\n\nFirst keep the original domain: $y>0$.\n\nRearranging once, we need\n\n$$\nf(c)=c^{2} \\ln{\\left(c \\right)} - e^{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- For $c=1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) &=- e^{2} + 1 \\ln{\\left(1 \\right)} \\\\ &=- e^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is nonzero, so it is not an equilibrium solution.\n\n- For $c=2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) &=- e^{2} + 4 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is nonzero, so it is not an equilibrium solution.\n\n- For $c=e$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(e) &=- e^{2} + e^{2} \\ln{\\left(e \\right)} \\\\ &=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is zero, so it is an equilibrium solution.\n\nSelect $y=e$.",
